WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:19.859 --> 00:00:23.800
- Hi everyone, welcome to the
Daily Homeroom live stream.

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:25.810
I'm Sal from Khan Academy,

00:00:25.810 --> 00:00:27.600
and I'm super excited
about our guest today.

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:29.430
So I'm actually just gonna
go through my announcements

00:00:29.430 --> 00:00:31.240
pretty fast so that we have as much time

00:00:31.240 --> 00:00:33.180
with Tom Friedman as possible.

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:34.340
But before we start,

00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:37.660
I do like to make my
standard announcement that,

00:00:37.660 --> 00:00:40.470
remember, Khan Academy is a not-for-profit

00:00:40.470 --> 00:00:42.640
we only exist through
philanthropic donations

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:43.910
from folks like yourself.

00:00:43.910 --> 00:00:45.700
And I do want to give a special shout out

00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:48.810
to several corporations that
stepped up when they found out

00:00:48.810 --> 00:00:52.160
that we needed to step up
more during this whole crisis.

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:54.730
Special thanks to Bank
of America, Google.org,

00:00:54.730 --> 00:00:57.868
AT&amp;T, fastly and Novartis, and
the many other corporations

00:00:57.868 --> 00:01:00.040
and foundations that
have been supporting us,

00:01:00.040 --> 00:01:01.520
even pre-COVID.

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:04.050
And we are running into deficit before,

00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:04.940
this has definitely helped,

00:01:04.940 --> 00:01:06.430
but we continue to run into deficit

00:01:06.430 --> 00:01:08.016
because of the traffic on our servers

00:01:08.016 --> 00:01:10.430
and all the programs that
we're trying to accelerate

00:01:10.430 --> 00:01:11.263
because of COVID.

00:01:11.263 --> 00:01:13.470
So if you are in a position to do so,

00:01:13.470 --> 00:01:15.900
please think about donating.

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:20.070
So with that, I'm super
excited to introduce our guest,

00:01:20.070 --> 00:01:22.090
Tom Friedman, I think
someone who doesn't need

00:01:22.090 --> 00:01:24.730
much of an introduction,
a famous columnist,

00:01:24.730 --> 00:01:26.440
an author from The New York Times,

00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:28.380
Tom, great to see you.

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:31.350
- Sal, great to be with
you, this is great.

00:01:31.350 --> 00:01:33.240
- So I'm glad you joined.

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:34.620
I don't think there's a lot to talk about,

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:36.320
not much interesting
going on in the world,

00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:38.637
but I figure, we're good at killing time.

00:01:38.637 --> 00:01:41.320
(laughs)

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:43.500
So where do we start?

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:45.570
I am curious.

00:01:45.570 --> 00:01:48.190
Your vantage point of all of the things

00:01:48.190 --> 00:01:50.660
that are now kind of have
been thrown up in the air

00:01:50.660 --> 00:01:53.760
from the healthcare crisis,
the economic crisis,

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:55.510
obviously even there's
an education crisis,

00:01:55.510 --> 00:01:57.290
maybe there's political stuff going on

00:01:57.290 --> 00:01:58.720
and obviously Khan Academy,

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:00.739
we're a non-political
organization, so we try to stay

00:02:00.739 --> 00:02:02.770
a little bit clear about
some of those conversations.

00:02:02.770 --> 00:02:04.070
But I'm curious,

00:02:04.070 --> 00:02:08.370
from your point of view as
a columnist and an author,

00:02:08.370 --> 00:02:10.130
you've written books about globalization,

00:02:10.130 --> 00:02:12.750
The World is Flat, all of these things.

00:02:12.750 --> 00:02:16.210
What do you think is the biggest aha!

00:02:16.210 --> 00:02:17.173
Of this moment?

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:22.560
- I think there are two
Sal, that really are shaping

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:24.383
how I cover this story.

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:30.360
The first is my youngest daughter,

00:02:30.460 --> 00:02:32.080
Natalie actually is the executive producer

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:34.460
of All Things Considered
weekend edition on NPR

00:02:34.460 --> 00:02:35.427
so I never miss it.

00:02:35.427 --> 00:02:38.640
And on Easter Sunday they did a roundup

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:41.088
of a pastor sermons
from around the country.

00:02:41.088 --> 00:02:44.580
And I particularly was struck

00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:47.300
by Michael Curry's sermon

00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:49.650
from the National Cathedral,

00:02:49.650 --> 00:02:52.083
because he ended it with a song.

00:02:53.070 --> 00:02:54.445
He started singing,

00:02:54.445 --> 00:02:59.356
♪ He's got the whole world in His hands ♪

00:02:59.356 --> 00:03:03.593
♪ He's got the whole
wide world in His hands ♪

00:03:03.593 --> 00:03:07.297
♪ He's got the whole world in His hands ♪

00:03:07.297 --> 00:03:11.964
♪ He's got the whole world in His hands ♪

00:03:13.340 --> 00:03:15.410
I think what is, for me--

00:03:15.410 --> 00:03:16.964
- Tom, you're pretty good singer

00:03:16.964 --> 00:03:19.170
- (laughs) I don't think so, but (mumbles)

00:03:19.170 --> 00:03:22.343
- That was ... Anyway,
but yeah, please continue.

00:03:22.343 --> 00:03:24.830
- What is the Uber story for me?

00:03:24.830 --> 00:03:28.530
So what has really shaped my
thinking more than anything

00:03:28.530 --> 00:03:33.530
about this story, is that
just substitute he for she.

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:38.640
For the first time Mother
Nature has us all in her hands.

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:43.640
For the first time our
generation of human species,

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:46.330
'cause I don't think
any of us are virtually,

00:03:46.330 --> 00:03:48.420
any of us were here in 1918,

00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:51.100
the last time she had us all in her hands,

00:03:51.100 --> 00:03:53.390
the last time there was a global pandemic.

00:03:53.390 --> 00:03:57.690
For the first time, we all
are facing one of nature's

00:03:57.690 --> 00:04:01.980
challenges together as a species.

00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:06.980
And Mother Nature throws
fastballs at us: droughts, floods,

00:04:07.370 --> 00:04:11.320
wildfires, germs, viruses, pandemics,

00:04:11.320 --> 00:04:15.870
and these other way ultimately
natural selection happens,

00:04:15.870 --> 00:04:17.080
for plants and animals.

00:04:17.080 --> 00:04:19.330
This is how she sorts out the fittest.

00:04:19.330 --> 00:04:22.150
And this is the first
time all of us together

00:04:22.150 --> 00:04:25.640
have faced this kind of
challenge, this kind of fastball.

00:04:25.640 --> 00:04:28.423
Now, so Mother Nature,

00:04:29.885 --> 00:04:32.370
one of the things you always
have to keep in mind is

00:04:32.370 --> 00:04:34.433
she actually doesn't reward the smartest.

00:04:35.550 --> 00:04:38.230
She also doesn't reward the strongest.

00:04:38.230 --> 00:04:40.710
She actually rewards the most adaptive

00:04:40.710 --> 00:04:42.580
when she throws her fastballs.

00:04:42.580 --> 00:04:45.390
Who can actually adapt best.

00:04:45.390 --> 00:04:49.650
And in the face of a pandemic,
she really is saying to us,

00:04:49.650 --> 00:04:52.990
"I reward three kinds of adaptation.

00:04:52.990 --> 00:04:55.140
First, I reward humility.

00:04:55.140 --> 00:04:57.420
Do you respect my virus?

00:04:57.420 --> 00:04:59.780
Because if you don't respect my virus,

00:04:59.780 --> 00:05:02.410
it will hurt you or someone you love.

00:05:02.410 --> 00:05:07.410
Second, are you coordinated
in facing my virus?

00:05:07.540 --> 00:05:10.630
Because I evolve my viruses over millennia

00:05:10.630 --> 00:05:13.310
to find any crack in your immune system.

00:05:13.310 --> 00:05:17.590
So unless you are really
coordinated in your response

00:05:17.590 --> 00:05:20.480
my virus will find that crack."

00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:24.260
And lastly, because Mother
Nature herself is just chemistry,

00:05:24.260 --> 00:05:26.877
biology and physics, she says,

00:05:26.877 --> 00:05:30.070
"I only reward adaptation strategies

00:05:30.070 --> 00:05:33.030
that are based on chemistry,
biology, and physics.

00:05:33.030 --> 00:05:36.150
If your adaptation strategy
is based on politics, ideology

00:05:36.150 --> 00:05:41.120
or an election schedule,
it won't be effective."

00:05:41.120 --> 00:05:43.880
And so for me Sal that's
kind of the Uber framework

00:05:43.880 --> 00:05:48.207
at which I look at everything,
I judge the responses

00:05:48.207 --> 00:05:52.453
and is the lens through
which I'm doing my analysis.

00:05:53.827 --> 00:05:55.880
- Actually there's a ton of
questions already coming in.

00:05:55.880 --> 00:05:56.736
I do wanna remind everyone watching

00:05:56.736 --> 00:05:59.810
if you have any questions
for, especially Tom,

00:05:59.810 --> 00:06:02.700
including me, feel free to put
them on the message boards,

00:06:02.700 --> 00:06:03.810
on YouTube and Facebook,

00:06:03.810 --> 00:06:06.630
we have team members who are
going to surface that to us.

00:06:06.630 --> 00:06:07.870
But given what you just said,

00:06:07.870 --> 00:06:09.500
which I think is a really
good way of thinking about it

00:06:09.500 --> 00:06:11.330
because so many times
where we're caught up

00:06:11.330 --> 00:06:13.900
in our human bubble, but
we are on this planet

00:06:13.900 --> 00:06:16.830
and there are forces
(chuckles) that can humble us

00:06:16.830 --> 00:06:18.610
very, very, very quickly.

00:06:18.610 --> 00:06:22.920
How do you think, well,
let me ask the question,

00:06:22.920 --> 00:06:24.470
how do you think this is gonna play out?

00:06:24.470 --> 00:06:26.120
How do you think it's gonna
play out in the short term?

00:06:26.120 --> 00:06:27.600
And what do you think are
gonna be, maybe the medium

00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:30.310
and longterm implications of all this?

00:06:30.310 --> 00:06:33.310
- Well, what I would
say is that what you see

00:06:33.310 --> 00:06:37.680
are three broad responses of adaptation

00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:39.383
playing out in the world today.

00:06:40.640 --> 00:06:44.730
So one, let's call it the
super efficient lockdown

00:06:46.920 --> 00:06:51.920
and then test, trace,
track and quarantine model.

00:06:52.960 --> 00:06:57.330
And probably the most super
efficient form of that

00:06:57.330 --> 00:07:00.900
is China 'cause it uses
basically a surveillance system

00:07:00.900 --> 00:07:03.980
that it uses for controlling a society

00:07:03.980 --> 00:07:06.773
to now track and trace the coronavirus.

00:07:08.124 --> 00:07:09.210
So that's one model.

00:07:09.210 --> 00:07:12.350
And then there are
democratic versions of that,

00:07:12.350 --> 00:07:15.170
Germany, South Korea, et cetera,

00:07:15.170 --> 00:07:17.533
and they've also been quite effective,

00:07:18.440 --> 00:07:21.820
Taiwan, New Zealand to name a few.

00:07:21.820 --> 00:07:25.260
At the other extreme you
have the model being ...

00:07:25.260 --> 00:07:27.060
And by the way, China is doing that,

00:07:27.060 --> 00:07:31.410
they hope to actually
completely quash COVID-19

00:07:31.410 --> 00:07:33.930
in their society and
the spread of transition

00:07:33.930 --> 00:07:36.729
until there is a vaccine.

00:07:36.729 --> 00:07:40.130
'Cause you only can
overcome a virus like this

00:07:40.130 --> 00:07:41.560
when there is herd immunity,

00:07:41.560 --> 00:07:43.700
and there's only two ways
to get herd immunity.

00:07:43.700 --> 00:07:45.260
One is through a vaccine

00:07:45.260 --> 00:07:48.825
and the other is by 60
to 70% of your population

00:07:48.825 --> 00:07:53.825
actually getting the infection
and overcoming it naturally

00:07:53.970 --> 00:07:55.321
by developing the antibodies.

00:07:55.321 --> 00:07:56.154
(phone ringing)

00:07:56.154 --> 00:07:57.444
Excuse me.

00:07:57.444 --> 00:07:58.505
- No worries.

00:07:58.505 --> 00:08:02.860
- So that's the China model.

00:08:02.860 --> 00:08:05.020
The second model is a Swedish model,

00:08:05.020 --> 00:08:07.170
and Sweden, well, they don't
like to say it directly,

00:08:07.170 --> 00:08:09.353
basically has gone for
natural herd immunity.

00:08:09.353 --> 00:08:12.530
Their model was actually
keep their economy open,

00:08:12.530 --> 00:08:14.500
close colleges and high schools,

00:08:14.500 --> 00:08:19.500
but let K-9 schools operate,
let malls and restaurants open

00:08:21.150 --> 00:08:23.500
but encourage social
distancing, mass squaring

00:08:23.500 --> 00:08:25.790
and protect the most vulnerable in theory,

00:08:25.790 --> 00:08:28.040
which they didn't do
very well in practice.

00:08:28.040 --> 00:08:30.680
Actually let the young
and the least vulnerable,

00:08:30.680 --> 00:08:33.633
those who will experience this
just as a mild flu likely,

00:08:34.886 --> 00:08:37.610
or tough flu but will not
require hospitalization

00:08:37.610 --> 00:08:39.850
to develop herd immunity.

00:08:39.850 --> 00:08:43.770
And bet that the number of
people who will get the disease

00:08:43.770 --> 00:08:47.800
and not require ICU units or ventilators

00:08:47.800 --> 00:08:51.160
will be less than sign
the number of ICU units

00:08:51.160 --> 00:08:51.993
and ventilators you have.

00:08:51.993 --> 00:08:53.410
That's the Swedish model.

00:08:53.410 --> 00:08:55.420
And the case is still out there,

00:08:55.420 --> 00:08:59.400
I mean, we just don't know if
they've had a lot more deaths

00:08:59.400 --> 00:09:00.650
than their neighbors.

00:09:00.650 --> 00:09:02.880
Obviously they're arguing
that the real test

00:09:02.880 --> 00:09:05.170
will be in three months,
whether they have herd immunity

00:09:05.170 --> 00:09:07.110
and they're done with this or not,

00:09:07.110 --> 00:09:09.876
but there's a lot of
controversy around that

00:09:09.876 --> 00:09:13.160
and there's simply no way
to judge at this stage.

00:09:13.160 --> 00:09:16.670
The third model is America,
and we're a mess, Sal.

00:09:16.670 --> 00:09:21.670
We basically, we talk
like we're gonna be China.

00:09:22.500 --> 00:09:26.292
We act like we're going
to be Sweden by default.

00:09:26.292 --> 00:09:30.480
We are really not preparing to do either,

00:09:30.480 --> 00:09:33.110
and our president's arguing
we're superior to both.

00:09:33.110 --> 00:09:35.550
So it's kind of a mess basically.

00:09:35.550 --> 00:09:39.310
And you have some states
where it's working well,

00:09:39.310 --> 00:09:41.700
others where it's working, not well,

00:09:41.700 --> 00:09:44.230
people who are wearing
masks, people who+ aren't.

00:09:44.230 --> 00:09:46.280
And I go back to Mother Nature

00:09:46.280 --> 00:09:48.560
'cause she actually doesn't know, Sal

00:09:48.560 --> 00:09:51.563
where the border between
Georgia and Florida is.

00:09:52.530 --> 00:09:57.530
And she really is not
interested in your lockdown

00:09:58.190 --> 00:10:01.070
or if you were tired of being locked down,

00:10:01.070 --> 00:10:04.323
she really is only gonna respect
your adaptation strategy.

00:10:06.710 --> 00:10:08.200
- I mean, so what do you think

00:10:08.200 --> 00:10:09.033
the implications are?

00:10:09.033 --> 00:10:10.090
I mean, just for the US and the world,

00:10:10.090 --> 00:10:13.410
I mean, even for a China or even a Sweden,

00:10:13.410 --> 00:10:15.056
well, I guess the herd immunity
could be very effective,

00:10:15.056 --> 00:10:17.670
but a place like China,
obviously at some point

00:10:17.670 --> 00:10:19.030
they're gonna wanna open up their borders.

00:10:19.030 --> 00:10:21.424
So even if they are able to
squash it in the short term

00:10:21.424 --> 00:10:23.700
to your point, Mother
Nature also does not know

00:10:23.700 --> 00:10:24.777
the borders of countries.

00:10:24.777 --> 00:10:28.690
If you just take a realist point of view,

00:10:28.690 --> 00:10:30.030
there's a world where you advocate

00:10:30.030 --> 00:10:31.600
for one policy or another.

00:10:31.600 --> 00:10:32.700
But if you just say like,

00:10:32.700 --> 00:10:34.810
the world is already on a trajectory,

00:10:34.810 --> 00:10:36.560
and there's gonna be political forces

00:10:36.560 --> 00:10:38.640
and economic forces at play,

00:10:38.640 --> 00:10:43.640
what do you see as the next
six to 12 months looking like?

00:10:44.330 --> 00:10:46.650
When you look at the stock
market, it looks like,

00:10:46.650 --> 00:10:49.033
at least the US stock market
looks like we're all clear

00:10:49.033 --> 00:10:52.040
(laughs) things are on ... I mean,

00:10:52.040 --> 00:10:55.130
what's your best guess
as a prognosticator?

00:10:55.130 --> 00:10:56.580
- Yeah, I read, an analyst
the other day said,

00:10:56.580 --> 00:11:00.230
the stock market's priced in
everything except a recession,

00:11:00.230 --> 00:11:03.570
related to the virus is quite remarkable,

00:11:03.570 --> 00:11:06.363
but it's in its own universe basically.

00:11:08.750 --> 00:11:09.820
What do I think is gonna happen,

00:11:09.820 --> 00:11:11.910
let me start with what
I'm praying will happen.

00:11:11.910 --> 00:11:15.460
I'm praying that Moore's
law will defeat COVID's law.

00:11:15.460 --> 00:11:18.967
That basically you gotta race
right now between Moore's law

00:11:18.967 --> 00:11:20.309
and COVID's law.

00:11:20.309 --> 00:11:23.930
COVID's law is about the speed
with which one single person

00:11:23.930 --> 00:11:26.800
can impact three others and
three others, three others,

00:11:26.800 --> 00:11:29.810
and how quickly this virus can spread.

00:11:29.810 --> 00:11:32.000
Moore's law says the speed
and power of microchips

00:11:32.000 --> 00:11:33.790
will double every 24 months.

00:11:33.790 --> 00:11:37.240
And so basically it's a proxy
for technological advance.

00:11:37.240 --> 00:11:40.530
And since 1918, Lord knows
we have technology tools now

00:11:40.530 --> 00:11:42.330
that we're applying to biology

00:11:42.330 --> 00:11:44.200
that just didn't exist before.

00:11:44.200 --> 00:11:49.200
And I'm hoping that they
will give us a vaccine

00:11:49.300 --> 00:11:51.080
or therapies at a minimum

00:11:51.080 --> 00:11:52.680
that will take death off the table.

00:11:52.680 --> 00:11:57.680
But a vaccine that by
the end of this year,

00:11:58.090 --> 00:12:01.230
early next year will allow
us to get through a vaccine

00:12:03.134 --> 00:12:05.215
a high degree of recurred
immunity around the world,

00:12:05.215 --> 00:12:06.363
that's my hope.

00:12:07.370 --> 00:12:09.720
Beyond that, it's really
outside my skillset.

00:12:09.720 --> 00:12:11.810
I listen to what the biologists say,

00:12:11.810 --> 00:12:14.359
the virologist, that they expect

00:12:14.359 --> 00:12:17.380
that we will get a second wave,

00:12:17.380 --> 00:12:18.730
we'll get a second wave in part

00:12:18.730 --> 00:12:21.480
because people just aren't practicing

00:12:21.480 --> 00:12:25.280
smart habits of social
distancing and masking

00:12:25.280 --> 00:12:27.493
and not collecting in groups,

00:12:28.960 --> 00:12:30.070
and we may get a second wave

00:12:30.070 --> 00:12:31.900
because the thing about this disease

00:12:31.900 --> 00:12:34.900
which makes it so beguiling is that,

00:12:34.900 --> 00:12:38.260
first of all, it comes in decaf,

00:12:38.260 --> 00:12:40.420
regular and double macchiato.

00:12:40.420 --> 00:12:42.720
And so first of all, many people get it

00:12:42.720 --> 00:12:44.430
and are completely asymptomatic,

00:12:44.430 --> 00:12:46.550
and the next person next to you gets it,

00:12:46.550 --> 00:12:48.740
and these are respirators.

00:12:48.740 --> 00:12:51.590
And so it clearly interacts
with different bodies,

00:12:51.590 --> 00:12:55.520
different DNA, different
comorbidities, different latitudes

00:12:55.520 --> 00:12:56.990
and different populations

00:12:56.990 --> 00:12:59.460
and different temperatures differently.

00:12:59.460 --> 00:13:02.710
And so I think you just
really have to respect it

00:13:02.710 --> 00:13:04.050
and be humble.

00:13:04.050 --> 00:13:06.680
And so I listen to what the biologists say

00:13:06.680 --> 00:13:08.069
and they're all basically telling us

00:13:08.069 --> 00:13:10.140
there's going to be a second wave

00:13:10.140 --> 00:13:12.400
and you listen to people
like the epidemiologist,

00:13:12.400 --> 00:13:14.810
Mike Osterholm from the
University of Minnesota

00:13:14.810 --> 00:13:18.780
and Tom Frieden, the
former head of the CD.C.,

00:13:18.780 --> 00:13:21.500
would just say that this thing
will just keep infecting us

00:13:21.500 --> 00:13:22.900
until it runs out of people.

00:13:23.914 --> 00:13:26.040
So unless we develop either natural

00:13:26.040 --> 00:13:28.470
or immunological herd immunity,

00:13:28.470 --> 00:13:31.770
so a vaccine or immunity.

00:13:31.770 --> 00:13:34.470
So I'm quite humble about it.

00:13:34.470 --> 00:13:38.493
Sal, I try not to get over my skis.

00:13:39.400 --> 00:13:40.820
- And what's your sense

00:13:40.820 --> 00:13:42.970
given even that somewhat hopeful picture

00:13:42.970 --> 00:13:45.960
of hopefully we can get a
vaccine near the end of the year,

00:13:45.960 --> 00:13:46.910
early next year.

00:13:46.910 --> 00:13:49.240
Let's talk about people being able to have

00:13:49.240 --> 00:13:51.210
some type of antiviral therapies,

00:13:51.210 --> 00:13:53.088
maybe sooner that to your point takes

00:13:53.088 --> 00:13:55.160
at least death off the table,

00:13:55.160 --> 00:13:58.540
but that still might be in
November timeframe type thing.

00:13:58.540 --> 00:14:01.920
What do you think is going to
happen from kind of a economic

00:14:01.920 --> 00:14:03.050
and societal point of view?

00:14:03.050 --> 00:14:04.460
'Cause even, we've seen

00:14:04.460 --> 00:14:07.130
what's happened to unemployment
just over the last six

00:14:07.130 --> 00:14:08.163
or eight weeks.

00:14:09.416 --> 00:14:11.560
What do you think is gonna
change about people's behavior,

00:14:11.560 --> 00:14:15.420
the economy, I don't wanna
get too much into the politics

00:14:15.420 --> 00:14:16.970
and who's doing right or wrong,

00:14:18.105 --> 00:14:21.003
but how might that
broadly affect politics?

00:14:22.590 --> 00:14:24.953
- Well, it's a really important
question you're asking.

00:14:26.270 --> 00:14:28.480
Let me just talk a little
bit about my own life,

00:14:28.480 --> 00:14:31.230
I'm talking to you from our
home in Bethesda, Maryland.

00:14:32.640 --> 00:14:35.810
My wife Ann's been building
a museum to promote reading

00:14:35.810 --> 00:14:36.736
and literacy in Washington D.C.

00:14:36.736 --> 00:14:39.793
it's called planning word,
planningwordmuseum.org.

00:14:41.026 --> 00:14:42.860
And we were about 90% done.

00:14:42.860 --> 00:14:45.400
We actually were supposed
to open next week,

00:14:45.400 --> 00:14:47.033
so that's been put on hold.

00:14:49.596 --> 00:14:52.050
But here's what I have noticed,

00:14:52.050 --> 00:14:55.720
so you and I are having a conversation now

00:14:56.670 --> 00:14:59.253
via this technology, is
Google technology platform.

00:15:00.400 --> 00:15:04.010
And I'd say Sal, it's about 85%

00:15:04.010 --> 00:15:06.290
is good as when I used
to come to your office,

00:15:06.290 --> 00:15:09.400
and you and I would sit
there across from your desk,

00:15:09.400 --> 00:15:12.710
but it's a 100% less stress on you and me,

00:15:12.710 --> 00:15:15.100
and we're reaching a wide audience.

00:15:15.100 --> 00:15:19.173
And so I have been having the most rich,

00:15:20.336 --> 00:15:24.960
deep Zoom conversations
now around the world.

00:15:26.670 --> 00:15:29.843
I started this morning
with a friend in Mumbai.

00:15:30.740 --> 00:15:33.620
I'm doing this now with
you, it's just a treat.

00:15:33.620 --> 00:15:37.035
And then this evening
I'm doing with a friend

00:15:37.035 --> 00:15:38.633
from a consulting firm.

00:15:39.760 --> 00:15:41.180
I had a day, a couple of weeks ago,

00:15:41.180 --> 00:15:44.093
where I did the India Today
platform in New Delhi.

00:15:45.100 --> 00:15:47.330
I then spoke to 82 UN ambassadors

00:15:47.330 --> 00:15:50.430
for the small ambassadors
coalition at the UN.

00:15:50.430 --> 00:15:54.290
And I ended the day with
the ballot form in Beijing.

00:15:54.290 --> 00:15:57.360
And I never left my seat here at home.

00:15:57.360 --> 00:16:01.980
And these were not facile interactions,

00:16:01.980 --> 00:16:04.520
They're actually, they felt deep.

00:16:04.520 --> 00:16:08.980
And so this will change my world.

00:16:08.980 --> 00:16:13.200
This will enable me to act globally.

00:16:13.200 --> 00:16:14.960
When I wrote The World Is Flat,

00:16:14.960 --> 00:16:17.173
what the book was
basically arguing is that,

00:16:19.170 --> 00:16:20.660
what made the world flat?

00:16:20.660 --> 00:16:22.700
The reason I was making that argument

00:16:22.700 --> 00:16:25.862
was that what we were seeing
was not globalization.

00:16:25.862 --> 00:16:28.820
In the old days to be global,
you had to be a country,

00:16:28.820 --> 00:16:31.700
and then the old days to be
global, you to be a company.

00:16:31.700 --> 00:16:32.550
What was new?

00:16:32.550 --> 00:16:34.200
That's what I was writing about

00:16:34.200 --> 00:16:36.510
when I wrote that book in 2005.

00:16:36.510 --> 00:16:40.730
What was new is the ability now
individuals to act globally,

00:16:40.730 --> 00:16:45.730
Spell and Tom are now
acting globally, okay.

00:16:46.310 --> 00:16:49.640
And as individuals, and I think that

00:16:49.640 --> 00:16:52.183
is only going to accelerate more and more.

00:16:53.091 --> 00:16:56.773
I wanna hear from you what
this means for education.

00:16:57.890 --> 00:16:59.970
And I've been talking to my
daughter early about that too.

00:16:59.970 --> 00:17:04.440
Obviously this is not going
away 'cause this technology

00:17:04.440 --> 00:17:06.780
is just going to get better and better.

00:17:06.780 --> 00:17:11.010
And I can only imagine so,

00:17:11.010 --> 00:17:12.950
but I do believe we're
gonna see more automation

00:17:12.950 --> 00:17:14.950
'cause machines don't infect each other,

00:17:16.290 --> 00:17:18.980
and we're going to see a
lot of companies realize,

00:17:18.980 --> 00:17:21.128
The New York Times has been closed

00:17:21.128 --> 00:17:24.430
from pretty much the first
week in March, we're closed.

00:17:24.430 --> 00:17:26.780
They announced we're not
coming back to the office

00:17:26.780 --> 00:17:30.920
till the end of summer, till
Labour Day, at the earliest.

00:17:30.920 --> 00:17:32.670
That includes the Washington Bureau I work

00:17:32.670 --> 00:17:34.940
and The New York Office.

00:17:34.940 --> 00:17:38.600
And I'm proud to say we are putting out

00:17:38.600 --> 00:17:42.040
the most amazing
newspaper every single day

00:17:42.040 --> 00:17:44.220
and none of us are in the office.

00:17:44.220 --> 00:17:46.914
Now, I don't know exactly
what the implications of that

00:17:46.914 --> 00:17:50.223
are gonna be, but they're
going to be implications.

00:17:51.510 --> 00:17:54.993
And I'm just trying to get
my mind around that now.

00:17:56.410 --> 00:17:57.770
- No, I mean, you're
touching on a lot of things.

00:17:57.770 --> 00:18:00.490
I have to say, a lot of people,
they start calls these days

00:18:00.490 --> 00:18:01.380
and say, "How are you doing?"

00:18:01.380 --> 00:18:03.804
And I say relatively speaking, "I'm fine."

00:18:03.804 --> 00:18:06.210
A lot of people are
suffering much more (mumbles)

00:18:06.210 --> 00:18:08.743
I almost feel guilt that to your point,

00:18:10.190 --> 00:18:11.100
there's aspects of this

00:18:11.100 --> 00:18:14.782
that I think have been a boon
(chuckles) and they've not--

00:18:14.782 --> 00:18:16.440
- (mumbles)

00:18:16.440 --> 00:18:17.273
- I've talked about it.

00:18:17.273 --> 00:18:19.530
I don't enjoy planes for
a whole bunch of reasons,

00:18:19.530 --> 00:18:21.210
I get claustrophobic on
them and all of that.

00:18:21.210 --> 00:18:22.910
But just the time, the energy

00:18:22.910 --> 00:18:25.029
and then being able to have
fairly good interactions \

00:18:25.029 --> 00:18:29.400
with folks all over the world like this

00:18:29.400 --> 00:18:30.770
I think it is a new world.

00:18:30.770 --> 00:18:32.870
And to your point, like
the New York Times,

00:18:32.870 --> 00:18:35.330
Khan Academy, we are already,
we're reasonably distributed,

00:18:35.330 --> 00:18:40.160
but even more so, the team
is as functional as ever,

00:18:40.160 --> 00:18:41.750
I mean, the main issue is

00:18:43.800 --> 00:18:45.600
the team members have child care issues

00:18:45.600 --> 00:18:46.760
while they're working from home.

00:18:46.760 --> 00:18:48.620
But if that wasn't an issue,

00:18:48.620 --> 00:18:50.270
I think a lot of folks are appreciating

00:18:50.270 --> 00:18:53.440
not having to commute, not
having to get on planes.

00:18:53.440 --> 00:18:57.940
And so it is a bit of a
brave new world for everyone

00:18:57.940 --> 00:19:00.070
that maybe we'll have some positives.

00:19:00.070 --> 00:19:01.550
- We're privileged I have a job,

00:19:01.550 --> 00:19:04.340
I work for a healthy
organization that laid me off.

00:19:04.340 --> 00:19:08.300
And so I understand
what a minority this is,

00:19:08.300 --> 00:19:11.130
but in terms of just
how these technologies

00:19:11.130 --> 00:19:13.520
are not gonna go away, I think they will,

00:19:13.520 --> 00:19:15.970
they're going to infuse
throughout our society,

00:19:15.970 --> 00:19:18.120
and the longer this goes on

00:19:18.120 --> 00:19:19.870
'cause the technologies will get better,

00:19:19.870 --> 00:19:24.870
the more remote learning,
remote work, remote lectures

00:19:25.630 --> 00:19:28.080
I think will become part of the norm.

00:19:28.080 --> 00:19:29.720
And I am curious about that last point

00:19:29.720 --> 00:19:32.230
'cause I think both you and
I we are very fortunate,

00:19:32.230 --> 00:19:34.283
we have jobs we can do remotely,

00:19:36.420 --> 00:19:37.410
which I think is maybe

00:19:37.410 --> 00:19:39.470
one of the most important
things right now.

00:19:39.470 --> 00:19:41.730
Given how many people,
especially in the service sector,

00:19:41.730 --> 00:19:45.590
in restaurants, airlines,
tourism, and now other things,

00:19:45.590 --> 00:19:47.710
people losing their jobs,

00:19:47.710 --> 00:19:51.170
record high unemployment in
our lifetimes, maybe ever,

00:19:51.170 --> 00:19:52.950
we don't know when this is gonna end.

00:19:52.950 --> 00:19:55.238
And to your point it might accelerate

00:19:55.238 --> 00:19:58.240
if factories lean even more on automation

00:19:58.240 --> 00:20:01.360
because you can't transmit
the virus and all of that,

00:20:01.360 --> 00:20:03.930
what's going to happen
to all of these folks?

00:20:03.930 --> 00:20:05.371
How are we going to be able to make sure

00:20:05.371 --> 00:20:08.070
that people stay engaged in society

00:20:08.070 --> 00:20:09.320
and they feel productive?

00:20:10.325 --> 00:20:13.300
And will it be temporary or
will it be somewhat structural?

00:20:13.300 --> 00:20:14.673
Even if we get the vaccine.

00:20:16.280 --> 00:20:17.710
One of the point Sal,

00:20:17.710 --> 00:20:21.610
I did a dialogue one day
with my friend Michael Sandel

00:20:21.610 --> 00:20:23.612
who teaches the justice course at Harvard,

00:20:23.612 --> 00:20:27.530
and we did a column together earlier on,

00:20:27.530 --> 00:20:30.320
what is the common good in a pandemic.

00:20:30.320 --> 00:20:31.570
Michael made this point,
it really struck me.

00:20:31.570 --> 00:20:36.570
And when you look around Sal
and look at all the people

00:20:36.660 --> 00:20:39.310
and all the jobs we are now depending on,

00:20:39.310 --> 00:20:41.700
people will go in and
stop grocery shelves,

00:20:41.700 --> 00:20:43.810
people will go in and butcher meat

00:20:43.810 --> 00:20:45.610
at meat packing factories,

00:20:45.610 --> 00:20:46.940
people who drive the truck

00:20:48.250 --> 00:20:51.020
from the meat packing
factory to the grocery store,

00:20:51.020 --> 00:20:52.895
the processing plant.

00:20:52.895 --> 00:20:55.350
All the people who ...

00:20:55.350 --> 00:20:57.440
Every crisis throws up different heroes,

00:20:57.440 --> 00:20:59.610
and 911, it was first responders,

00:20:59.610 --> 00:21:01.450
and this one is first responders too.

00:21:01.450 --> 00:21:04.623
But now it's all these
people who do traditionally,

00:21:06.594 --> 00:21:09.050
it wouldn't be wrong to
say we're looked down upon,

00:21:09.050 --> 00:21:11.560
do your homework, study Khan Academy.

00:21:11.560 --> 00:21:13.260
so you don't have to be a truck driver.

00:21:13.260 --> 00:21:15.920
It would have been a cliche,
you would have heard out there.

00:21:15.920 --> 00:21:17.890
These people are heroes now.

00:21:17.890 --> 00:21:20.090
They're actually the ones saving us.

00:21:20.090 --> 00:21:21.840
And by going to work,

00:21:21.840 --> 00:21:23.390
they're actually risking their lives.

00:21:23.390 --> 00:21:24.406
Think of someone who
sits in a grocery store

00:21:24.406 --> 00:21:27.370
all day, stocking shelves,

00:21:27.370 --> 00:21:28.470
and we need to find a way

00:21:28.470 --> 00:21:31.230
to honor these people after this is over.

00:21:31.230 --> 00:21:33.280
My friend Dov Seidman says,

00:21:33.280 --> 00:21:36.470
right now we're busy saving people,

00:21:36.470 --> 00:21:38.320
but after this leaders need to figure out

00:21:38.320 --> 00:21:39.700
how to serve people.

00:21:39.700 --> 00:21:41.900
We need to make a pivot after this

00:21:41.900 --> 00:21:45.410
and make sure that these people

00:21:46.983 --> 00:21:50.730
have the kind of health care,
have the kind of family care,

00:21:50.730 --> 00:21:54.033
child care and access to
education that they deserve,

00:21:54.033 --> 00:21:56.503
that everyone in our society deserves.

00:21:58.005 --> 00:22:00.710
And that it's really got
me certainly thinking

00:22:00.710 --> 00:22:03.420
a lot more about that.

00:22:03.420 --> 00:22:07.510
And I've never been a big fan
of universal basic income only

00:22:07.510 --> 00:22:10.420
'cause I think dignity is so much derived

00:22:10.420 --> 00:22:12.150
from work and working,

00:22:12.150 --> 00:22:16.317
but the way we've been dividing the pie

00:22:16.317 --> 00:22:18.530
is not sustainable.

00:22:18.530 --> 00:22:23.150
And out of this I think
needs to come a real,

00:22:23.150 --> 00:22:25.313
a different division of the pie,

00:22:26.630 --> 00:22:28.420
more for labor than for capital

00:22:29.630 --> 00:22:32.950
and safety nets that are reward and honor

00:22:32.950 --> 00:22:37.700
these people who we now
find out are so important

00:22:37.700 --> 00:22:39.450
for the functioning of our society.

00:22:40.650 --> 00:22:43.080
- And what's your sense, there's
a bunch of questions here

00:22:43.080 --> 00:22:44.130
from Facebook and YouTube.

00:22:44.130 --> 00:22:45.490
Tim Kearney asks,

00:22:45.490 --> 00:22:48.519
he's referring to your
kind of Darwinian frame

00:22:48.519 --> 00:22:49.564
that you started with.

00:22:49.564 --> 00:22:51.530
If COVID-19 is survival of the fittest,

00:22:51.530 --> 00:22:53.350
what responsibility does
the Federal Government have

00:22:53.350 --> 00:22:54.720
to support the least fit?

00:22:54.720 --> 00:22:56.850
And I'll add a little bit
to it 'cause this takes,

00:22:56.850 --> 00:22:59.030
I would say maybe a health
angle, the least fit,

00:22:59.030 --> 00:23:01.430
but also as we just touched
about the economically

00:23:01.430 --> 00:23:05.450
vulnerable as well, and there
is this really hard tension

00:23:05.450 --> 00:23:08.820
that you might save
people with the shutdowns

00:23:08.820 --> 00:23:11.760
on the COVID side, but it
might lead to more poverty,

00:23:11.760 --> 00:23:13.960
more unemployment, hunger,

00:23:13.960 --> 00:23:16.770
and all sorts of forms
of death are correlated

00:23:16.770 --> 00:23:19.250
with not having a job
and not having income.

00:23:19.250 --> 00:23:21.660
So how does someone weigh that?

00:23:21.660 --> 00:23:23.330
Well, how do you thread that needle?

00:23:23.330 --> 00:23:26.070
- It's really important
question and I would say,

00:23:26.070 --> 00:23:28.660
from the very beginning
and in the beginning

00:23:28.660 --> 00:23:30.420
it was controversial.

00:23:30.420 --> 00:23:33.010
I was one of the very first people writing

00:23:33.010 --> 00:23:37.197
that the strategy we need is one

00:23:37.197 --> 00:23:41.540
of what Dr. David Cass called
total harm minimization.

00:23:41.540 --> 00:23:44.370
How do we maximize saving lives

00:23:44.370 --> 00:23:46.080
and saving livelihoods?

00:23:46.080 --> 00:23:48.690
Because there's a lot of ways to die,

00:23:48.690 --> 00:23:51.560
you can die immediately by COVID-19,

00:23:51.560 --> 00:23:55.010
and you can die longterm
by a death of despair,

00:23:55.010 --> 00:23:58.490
of unemployment, of
loss of hope and income.

00:23:58.490 --> 00:24:02.111
And both are thrown up by this fires.

00:24:02.111 --> 00:24:03.667
Sometimes people do say,

00:24:03.667 --> 00:24:05.700
"Whoa, that sounds really Darwinian."

00:24:05.700 --> 00:24:07.081
Guess what, it is.

00:24:07.081 --> 00:24:10.860
When we're all in the grip
of Mother Nature, it is,

00:24:10.860 --> 00:24:13.270
there are no easy solutions.

00:24:13.270 --> 00:24:15.250
And the point is to respect to me

00:24:15.250 --> 00:24:17.380
both sides of that equation.

00:24:17.380 --> 00:24:19.500
How do we maximize saving lives?

00:24:19.500 --> 00:24:22.900
Maximize saving livelihoods?

00:24:22.900 --> 00:24:25.240
And it seem to me the way you do that,

00:24:25.240 --> 00:24:28.210
and this was what people like
Carts were talking about,

00:24:28.210 --> 00:24:30.440
Amnon Shashoua in Israel.

00:24:30.440 --> 00:24:32.610
Basically you're locked down,

00:24:32.610 --> 00:24:34.660
you break the chains of transmission,

00:24:34.660 --> 00:24:37.120
you protect the most vulnerable.

00:24:37.120 --> 00:24:39.620
You literally don't let
them go out and you pointed

00:24:39.620 --> 00:24:40.820
and sequester them.

00:24:40.820 --> 00:24:43.260
And then on a risk stratified basis,

00:24:43.260 --> 00:24:45.030
you feed back into the workforce.

00:24:45.030 --> 00:24:47.890
The people who are most likely
to experience the virus,

00:24:47.890 --> 00:24:52.060
either asymptomatically
or as a mild or tough flu,

00:24:52.060 --> 00:24:53.436
not require hospitalization

00:24:53.436 --> 00:24:55.830
and let them keep the economy running

00:24:55.830 --> 00:24:57.700
or get it running again,

00:24:57.700 --> 00:25:00.180
it had to be that kind of a strategy.

00:25:00.180 --> 00:25:01.980
One of the big problems we have Sal,

00:25:01.980 --> 00:25:05.790
and I'm not gonna get into
politics here any more

00:25:05.790 --> 00:25:10.790
than I need to, but we haven't
had a national navigator.

00:25:11.290 --> 00:25:15.240
And so basically the public
here has been caught between

00:25:15.240 --> 00:25:18.700
what I call breaking news
and president breaking bad.

00:25:18.700 --> 00:25:21.760
Okay, so you're watching the
news and suddenly breaks in

00:25:21.760 --> 00:25:25.350
and tells you that 24
kids in the New York area

00:25:25.350 --> 00:25:30.350
experience COVID-19 is dramatic
and dangerous information.

00:25:30.828 --> 00:25:35.144
But what you're not told is
that 24 to 24, 24 to 240,

00:25:35.144 --> 00:25:40.144
24 to 2,400, 24 to 240,000,

00:25:40.660 --> 00:25:42.990
if there's one lesson,
I hope you're teaching,

00:25:42.990 --> 00:25:45.810
it's the difference between
denominator and numerator.

00:25:45.810 --> 00:25:48.340
And when you know what the denominator is

00:25:48.340 --> 00:25:50.430
and breaking news is constantly in there

00:25:50.430 --> 00:25:53.500
hitting you with breaking
news that a 24 year old

00:25:53.500 --> 00:25:55.160
died of a stroke or whatever.

00:25:55.160 --> 00:25:57.530
And if you don't know
what is the denominator,

00:25:57.530 --> 00:26:00.530
what is the numerator, how
do I put this in perspective?

00:26:00.530 --> 00:26:01.910
So you have that on one side

00:26:01.910 --> 00:26:04.230
and then you have a president
who's not wearing a mask

00:26:04.230 --> 00:26:07.510
using hydroxychloroquine
that its own FDA rejects

00:26:07.510 --> 00:26:10.774
and putting out crazy ideas about Lysol

00:26:10.774 --> 00:26:12.760
and ultraviolet light.

00:26:12.760 --> 00:26:16.570
And so we have not had,
like New Zealand has had

00:26:16.570 --> 00:26:21.570
a calm voice at the top,
educating you, uniting us,

00:26:21.720 --> 00:26:23.530
making clear that we're
all in this together.

00:26:23.530 --> 00:26:28.050
So don't go to a restaurant without a mask

00:26:28.050 --> 00:26:30.890
or crowded in there,
that's not a sign of macho,

00:26:30.890 --> 00:26:33.610
that's a sign of disrespect
to your neighbor,

00:26:33.610 --> 00:26:35.663
to your family, to your grandparents.

00:26:36.700 --> 00:26:39.270
This is not us against each other,

00:26:39.270 --> 00:26:41.470
This is us against all of us,

00:26:41.470 --> 00:26:44.080
against Mother Nature's fastball.

00:26:44.080 --> 00:26:46.323
And we really need to
think of it that way.

00:26:48.170 --> 00:26:50.030
- Now, and there are a lot
more questions coming in

00:26:50.030 --> 00:26:52.660
on Facebook, these are two
related, Roger Stevens asks,

00:26:52.660 --> 00:26:54.080
what are the biggest societal changes

00:26:54.080 --> 00:26:55.860
you think we'll see
coming out of the pandemic

00:26:55.860 --> 00:26:58.220
and Tim Kearney, I teach first grade,

00:26:58.220 --> 00:26:59.960
what type of long-term
changes do you foresee

00:26:59.960 --> 00:27:01.780
for my colleagues and students?

00:27:01.780 --> 00:27:06.580
How are they better or
are they just necessary?

00:27:06.580 --> 00:27:08.120
How will they be temporary
versus new normal?

00:27:08.120 --> 00:27:09.152
So it's really two questions of

00:27:09.152 --> 00:27:11.520
what's gonna be the new norm?

00:27:11.520 --> 00:27:12.353
And we talked a little bit,

00:27:12.353 --> 00:27:13.650
maybe the nature of work changes,

00:27:13.650 --> 00:27:17.370
maybe a lot fewer people
slept into the office

00:27:17.370 --> 00:27:19.590
with that long commute sitting in traffic,

00:27:19.590 --> 00:27:24.100
maybe we travel less,
which frankly I would love.

00:27:24.100 --> 00:27:27.420
What are other things that
you think might happen?

00:27:27.420 --> 00:27:30.901
- So, one of the things, I've
been a journalist (mumbles)

00:27:30.901 --> 00:27:34.630
since 1979, so it was my 40th year,

00:27:34.630 --> 00:27:36.653
and actually in '78 I started.

00:27:38.070 --> 00:27:41.710
And so if I look back at my career,

00:27:41.710 --> 00:27:44.670
the stories I'd most like to take back,

00:27:44.670 --> 00:27:48.320
the stories in columns
began with this sentence,

00:27:48.320 --> 00:27:50.270
the world will never be the same again.

00:27:51.610 --> 00:27:53.950
That's a dangerous sentence.

00:27:53.950 --> 00:27:57.653
So I'm humble about that
too, I wanna be very careful.

00:27:58.540 --> 00:28:02.930
But what it's clear to me
is pandemics to accelerate

00:28:02.930 --> 00:28:07.310
existing trends and they often

00:28:07.310 --> 00:28:10.110
exacerbate existing fractures.

00:28:10.110 --> 00:28:12.270
And clearly they are going to,

00:28:12.270 --> 00:28:16.370
this one is going to
accelerate distance learning,

00:28:16.370 --> 00:28:19.720
distance work and replacing travel

00:28:19.720 --> 00:28:22.920
with this kind of
teleporting, telecommuting,

00:28:22.920 --> 00:28:26.150
telemedicine that I think
is just gonna become

00:28:26.150 --> 00:28:29.890
more and more effective and efficient.

00:28:29.890 --> 00:28:34.060
And so I mean, I've
seen Zoom presentations.

00:28:34.060 --> 00:28:35.840
I know you can do it on
this Google platform too

00:28:35.840 --> 00:28:39.793
now with graphics, and it's
just gonna get so good.

00:28:40.971 --> 00:28:45.423
That clearly that's
gonna be an Uber trend.

00:28:46.890 --> 00:28:51.380
Beyond that, it seems to
me another broad trend,

00:28:51.380 --> 00:28:55.980
and this is a phrase that
my friend Seidman uses.

00:28:55.980 --> 00:28:58.751
First, we work with our
hands for generations,

00:28:58.751 --> 00:29:02.103
millennial, then we work with our heads,

00:29:03.950 --> 00:29:06.340
computing the information economy,

00:29:06.340 --> 00:29:09.840
and going forward more we're
gonna work with our hearts,

00:29:09.840 --> 00:29:13.373
we're gonna work with things
that connect people-to-people,

00:29:14.280 --> 00:29:17.230
and that require
people-to-people connections

00:29:17.230 --> 00:29:19.830
and enhance people-to-people connections.

00:29:19.830 --> 00:29:22.740
So I have a motto, if you're
not in the community business,

00:29:22.740 --> 00:29:26.500
you're not in business today,
that is in the retail sense.

00:29:26.500 --> 00:29:29.133
If you're not creating
a community experience,

00:29:31.070 --> 00:29:32.810
you are just the next company

00:29:32.810 --> 00:29:35.203
that's gonna be devoured by Amazon.

00:29:36.770 --> 00:29:40.820
Because if all you have
is a price arbitrage,

00:29:40.820 --> 00:29:42.040
there's going to be somebody bigger

00:29:42.040 --> 00:29:44.063
and more efficient at that.

00:29:44.063 --> 00:29:46.930
So things that connect people-to-people,

00:29:46.930 --> 00:29:48.880
like I really love what we're doing,

00:29:48.880 --> 00:29:50.500
but I will love going out of this

00:29:50.500 --> 00:29:53.590
and sitting around in
my backyard with friends

00:29:53.590 --> 00:29:55.402
who can come over six feet apart

00:29:55.402 --> 00:29:58.300
even more after I've
spent a day doing this.

00:29:58.300 --> 00:30:01.530
So I just think that it's
gonna be more and more work

00:30:01.530 --> 00:30:03.643
of connecting hearts-to-hearts,

00:30:04.510 --> 00:30:09.480
and the machines will
be doing the head stuff.

00:30:09.480 --> 00:30:12.510
My friend Ravi Kumar who
runs Mphasis actually,

00:30:15.223 --> 00:30:16.893
the Indian high-tech firm.

00:30:18.420 --> 00:30:20.490
Ravi basically says in the future

00:30:21.920 --> 00:30:24.880
machines will do the problem solving

00:30:24.880 --> 00:30:27.713
and people will do the problem finding.

00:30:28.994 --> 00:30:31.500
And that's why Mphasis for instance

00:30:31.500 --> 00:30:35.150
is hiring fewer STEM people
and more anthropologists

00:30:35.150 --> 00:30:36.910
and psych (mumbles).

00:30:36.910 --> 00:30:39.977
People who can find problems.

00:30:39.977 --> 00:30:42.540
That was Steve Jobs, he's genius,

00:30:42.540 --> 00:30:44.430
he knew you wanted an iPhone

00:30:44.430 --> 00:30:49.430
before you knew it and he's
the great example of that.

00:30:49.520 --> 00:30:53.200
But problem finders I think
are gonna be in great demand

00:30:53.200 --> 00:30:56.090
and then we'll let the AI
and the machine and the bot

00:30:56.090 --> 00:30:57.340
be the problem solvers.

00:30:57.340 --> 00:30:59.950
I think that shift is gonna happen more.

00:30:59.950 --> 00:31:01.992
It's something you might wanna
be thinking about it at con,

00:31:01.992 --> 00:31:04.690
you know what I mean?

00:31:04.690 --> 00:31:07.120
We surely, look, you can't
be a good problem finder

00:31:07.120 --> 00:31:09.670
if you don't know math
and you aren't literate

00:31:09.670 --> 00:31:12.600
and you don't know history and whatnot,

00:31:12.600 --> 00:31:17.600
but ultimately, is that a
vein for con to be mining,

00:31:19.770 --> 00:31:21.523
the idea of problem finding.

00:31:22.510 --> 00:31:24.990
It'd be interesting to have
a course in problem finding.

00:31:24.990 --> 00:31:27.150
- Yeah, I've never
thought about it that way,

00:31:27.150 --> 00:31:29.640
but it makes a lot of sense
that the problem definition

00:31:29.640 --> 00:31:30.990
and recognizing there's a need.

00:31:30.990 --> 00:31:33.120
Now that makes, and then
maybe on that last point,

00:31:33.120 --> 00:31:36.140
'cause it does kind of touch
on careers of the future

00:31:36.140 --> 00:31:38.540
and all of that, I've been
having a lot of conversations

00:31:38.540 --> 00:31:41.727
with young people, it's graduation time,

00:31:41.727 --> 00:31:44.090
you can imagine there's a lot of anxiety,

00:31:44.090 --> 00:31:45.450
there's always anxiety of that age.

00:31:45.450 --> 00:31:46.610
But especially in this,

00:31:46.610 --> 00:31:48.900
we have a couple of questions from folks.

00:31:48.900 --> 00:31:51.701
Ashley Paskal sounds like
she's a young journalist,

00:31:51.701 --> 00:31:54.790
how should she navigate
her career during COVID-19?

00:31:54.790 --> 00:31:56.870
But I'll broaden the question,

00:31:56.870 --> 00:31:58.967
what advice do you have for young people

00:31:58.967 --> 00:32:03.967
entering the world into a
very, very tough job market,

00:32:04.210 --> 00:32:08.090
a world where things
are an incredible flux.

00:32:08.090 --> 00:32:09.890
What advice would you have for them?

00:32:15.230 --> 00:32:19.030
- I guess one of the
things that I said earlier,

00:32:19.030 --> 00:32:22.690
this exact survey, it's current trends.

00:32:22.690 --> 00:32:25.397
So if you are, for instance,

00:32:25.397 --> 00:32:28.680
an established journalists,
so I can just call some up

00:32:28.680 --> 00:32:32.370
and say that "Zoom" and you can call me up

00:32:32.370 --> 00:32:33.480
and say, "Tom, let's just Zoom in"

00:32:33.480 --> 00:32:34.320
And bam, we do it."

00:32:34.320 --> 00:32:35.310
It's like that.

00:32:35.310 --> 00:32:37.830
But I pity someone who can't do that.

00:32:37.830 --> 00:32:40.210
So Zoom is only good if I can call you up

00:32:40.210 --> 00:32:42.680
and you will be the counterparty.

00:32:42.680 --> 00:32:47.343
But this is such a challenging
time to start out in,

00:32:48.280 --> 00:32:52.150
at the same time 'cause these
things always go both ways.

00:32:52.150 --> 00:32:53.940
It's like a whole new world.

00:32:53.940 --> 00:32:58.940
And there's such virgin territory
there to be reported on,

00:32:58.991 --> 00:33:03.540
so many motherloads of
emotion and psychology

00:33:03.540 --> 00:33:04.860
now playing out.

00:33:04.860 --> 00:33:08.770
And if you can find a
platform to publish on,

00:33:08.770 --> 00:33:12.300
an online platform or
somehow create your own,

00:33:12.300 --> 00:33:14.030
you don't have to be
at the New York Times,

00:33:14.030 --> 00:33:17.220
you can break in now, like never before.

00:33:17.220 --> 00:33:21.873
And so my advice always for
young journalists is that,

00:33:23.230 --> 00:33:26.330
is write, write, write
and write some more.

00:33:26.330 --> 00:33:29.910
That is the only way you
learn how to be a journalist,

00:33:29.910 --> 00:33:33.280
is to write and develop
a mountain of clips

00:33:33.280 --> 00:33:36.603
that show how you can write
the issues you can take on,

00:33:38.535 --> 00:33:41.210
the stories you can frame and find.,

00:33:41.210 --> 00:33:43.810
and that's how you build a reputation.

00:33:43.810 --> 00:33:46.650
But there's no other way,

00:33:46.650 --> 00:33:50.043
I mean, going to journalism
school now through Zoom,

00:33:51.070 --> 00:33:53.080
doesn't strike me as very productive.

00:33:53.080 --> 00:33:58.080
So I have a lot of sympathy
for people starting out now.

00:33:59.690 --> 00:34:01.540
My mind's been elsewhere, frankly,

00:34:01.540 --> 00:34:02.770
for the last couple of months

00:34:02.770 --> 00:34:04.964
but I need to think more about that,

00:34:04.964 --> 00:34:06.510
it's a very good question.

00:34:06.510 --> 00:34:08.940
But the most important thing you can do,

00:34:08.940 --> 00:34:11.649
the thing that always works
is write and get published.

00:34:11.649 --> 00:34:13.160
And by the way, it doesn't matter

00:34:13.160 --> 00:34:15.850
whether it's for your
neighborhood shopper,

00:34:15.850 --> 00:34:20.200
your local community newspaper,
your blog, your online,

00:34:20.200 --> 00:34:23.140
just something that can show your talent,

00:34:23.140 --> 00:34:26.037
that you can show someone
else for them to say,

00:34:26.037 --> 00:34:30.887
"Hey, that was pretty good,
I'm gonna give you a chance."

00:34:32.060 --> 00:34:33.153
- And I'll add to that.

00:34:33.153 --> 00:34:36.480
I'm not a journalist, you
very much are a journalist,

00:34:36.480 --> 00:34:37.840
but some of the stuff that you pointed out

00:34:37.840 --> 00:34:39.230
is stuff that I felt too, is that,

00:34:39.230 --> 00:34:40.654
there's all this reporting in the media,

00:34:40.654 --> 00:34:43.871
but sometimes people are
like losing the meat of it,

00:34:43.871 --> 00:34:46.780
they're reporting, this
state has this many cases,

00:34:46.780 --> 00:34:47.613
that state has (mumbles).

00:34:47.613 --> 00:34:48.570
Well, what's the per capita?

00:34:48.570 --> 00:34:51.010
Like how at risk will I be?

00:34:51.010 --> 00:34:52.690
They're losing that, yes,

00:34:52.690 --> 00:34:54.940
there is a young person
who had this symptom

00:34:54.940 --> 00:34:57.760
and it's really scary,
but give the context of,

00:34:57.760 --> 00:35:01.440
guess what, there's always
been one in a million cases

00:35:01.440 --> 00:35:03.070
of really horrible things happening.

00:35:03.070 --> 00:35:06.840
So that might not be that as
different as the media implies.

00:35:06.840 --> 00:35:08.890
So whether someone's
going to get into starting

00:35:08.890 --> 00:35:11.560
in journalism or any other field,

00:35:11.560 --> 00:35:13.630
because everything is thrown
up in the air right now.

00:35:13.630 --> 00:35:16.410
There are opportunities
to maybe find niches,

00:35:16.410 --> 00:35:18.620
just be kind of an evolutionary terms

00:35:18.620 --> 00:35:20.430
that might not have been there before.

00:35:20.430 --> 00:35:22.790
Our ancestors wouldn't have
come out of their holes

00:35:22.790 --> 00:35:25.540
if the dinosaurs weren't hit
by (laughs) the meteorite.

00:35:29.010 --> 00:35:31.284
So Tom, thank you so much for this.

00:35:31.284 --> 00:35:32.830
Whenever we have a good conversation

00:35:32.830 --> 00:35:34.860
the time goes unusually fast.

00:35:34.860 --> 00:35:37.810
We're already over time,
but I really appreciate this

00:35:37.810 --> 00:35:38.840
and I hope we can do this again

00:35:38.840 --> 00:35:40.070
and we can have more of these

00:35:40.070 --> 00:35:42.416
highly stimulating Zoom conversations.

00:35:42.416 --> 00:35:44.040
- Absolutely Sal.

00:35:44.040 --> 00:35:44.960
Thank you very much.

00:35:44.960 --> 00:35:48.334
Thanks to all the people who
tuned in, I really enjoyed it.

00:35:48.334 --> 00:35:49.821
- Thanks so much.

00:35:49.821 --> 00:35:50.654
- Goodbye.

00:35:51.630 --> 00:35:52.750
- So everyone, thanks again.

00:35:52.750 --> 00:35:53.890
As you can tell, these conversations

00:35:53.890 --> 00:35:55.730
are always super fun for myself.

00:35:55.730 --> 00:35:57.800
Hopefully you'll have enjoyed it as well.

00:35:57.800 --> 00:35:59.490
I've actually, the reason
why I'm in the office,

00:35:59.490 --> 00:36:01.460
is the socially distance, I'm
the only one in the office.

00:36:01.460 --> 00:36:03.670
I don't think they've turned
the air conditioner on today.

00:36:03.670 --> 00:36:04.770
So that's why I might be looking

00:36:04.770 --> 00:36:07.240
a little bit more shiny than normal.

00:36:07.240 --> 00:36:10.840
But it's always incredibly
stimulating for myself,

00:36:10.840 --> 00:36:13.610
and I look forward to
seeing you'll in future,

00:36:13.610 --> 00:36:15.840
I guess we could call them episodes.

00:36:15.840 --> 00:36:17.370
Tomorrow, it'll be just me,

00:36:17.370 --> 00:36:21.080
and I'm happy to take any
questions you have about anything.

00:36:21.080 --> 00:36:23.230
So with that, I will
see everyone tomorrow.

